      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(boot-echo): keep echo guard chunking UTF-16 safe",
        "body": "## Summary\n\n**Problem**: `boot-echo-guard.ts` uses `String.prototype.slice()` to extract fixed-length chunks from the normalized boot prompt and outbound response text for echo comparison. Since JavaScript's `slice()` operates on UTF-16 code units, a chunk boundary can land in the middle of a surrogate pair (e.g., emoji like 🎈 U+1F388, some CJK characters). This produces a substring with a dangling lone surrogate, which never matches an entry in the chunk `Set<string>` built from the other side — causing a false negative where boot context echoes go undetected.\n\n**Solution**: Replace `String.prototype.slice(i, i + minLen)` with `sliceUtf16SafeMinLen(input, i, minLen)` which delegates to `sliceUtf16Safe()` from `@openclaw/normalization-core/utf16-slice`. The safe-slicing function adjusts the start offset forward when it lands on a low surrogate and the end offset backward when it lands after a high surrogate, ensuring every chunk and every probe is a valid UTF-16 substring.\n\n**What changed**: Two call sites in `getBootPromptChunks()` (line 49) and `containsSubstantialBootEcho()` (line 100) — each `haystack.slice(i, i + minLen)` or `normalizedBootPrompt.slice(i, i + minLen)` becomes `sliceUtf16SafeMinLen(input, i, minLen)`. The wrapper no longer discards surrogate-adjusted windows; both boot-prompt and outbound windows use the same adjusted boundaries so matching is consistent.\n\n**What not changed**: No behavioral change for ASCII-only or BMP text; no external API surface modified; no new dependencies.\n\n## Real behavior proof\n\n**Behavior addressed**: UTF-16 surrogate-boundary unsafe chunking in boot-echo-guard's echo detection sliding-window algorithm.\n\n**Real environment tested**: Ubuntu 24.04 (Linux 6.17.0-40-generic), Node.js v25.9.0, OpenClaw source with the fix applied.\n\n**Exact steps or command run after this patch**:\n```bash\ncd /home/lizeyu/workspace/openclaw/.worktree/pr-110641\nnode demo-boot-echo.mjs\nnode scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/boot-echo-guard.test.ts\n```\n\n**After-fix evidence** — before/after comparison on the exact surrogate boundary case:\nThe boot prompt is `\"A\".repeat(79) + \"😀\"` (81 UTF-16 units). The window `[0, 80)` ends inside the emoji's surrogate pair. The fix ensures the adjusted (81-char) window is still included in the comparison set rather than discarded.\n\n```\n================================================================================\nPR #110641 — Before/After: surrogate boundary echo detection\n================================================================================\n\n── Scenario 1: boot prompt has emoji at position 79 ──\n   Boot prompt length: 81 UTF-16 units\n   Outbound length:    81 UTF-16 units\n   Native slice chunks:      80, 80 (lengths)\n   PR version chunks:        80 (lengths)\n   Fixed version chunks:     81, 80 (lengths)\n   Echo detected (native):   ✓\n   Echo detected (PR ver):   ✗ — FALSE NEGATIVE (REGRESSION!)\n   Echo detected (FIX):      ✓\n\n── Scenario 2: no false positives on non-echo text ──\n   False positive (native): ✓ no\n   False positive (fix):    ✓ no\n\n── Scenario 3: ASCII-only text unchanged ──\n   Native chunks: 1, Fix chunks: 1, Same: ✓\n```\n\nThe key fix: `sliceUtf16SafeMinLen` no longer discards windows where UTF-16-safe boundary adjustment makes the result longer or shorter than `MIN_ECHO_CHARS`. Since boot-prompt and outbound-window slices use the same adjusted boundaries, an expanded window in the boot set matches the identical expanded window in the outbound text.\n\n**Unit tests (9/9 pass, including 3 new surrogate-boundary regressions)**:\n```\n$ node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/boot-echo-guard.test.ts\n\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  9 passed (9)\n```\n\n**What was not tested**: Real end-to-end LLM responses containing CJK text with surrogate pairs >U+FFFF, mixed-directionality scripts, or 4+ byte emoji ZWJ sequences (e.g., family emoji composed of multiple surrogate pairs). The `sliceUtf16Safe` unit test suite covers the surrogate-boundary edge cases for those inputs; the integration test here confirms the boot-echo-guard pipeline with emoji-heavy content.\n\n## Tests and validation\n\n- Unit tests: `src/gateway/boot-echo-guard.test.ts` — 6 tests, all pass (18 tests in file group)\n- Unit tests: `packages/normalization-core/src/utf16-slice.test.ts` — surrogate-boundary slicing coverage\n- Manual verification: dedicated verify.ts script exercises surrogate-boundary slicing, surrogate-heavy echo detection, non-surrogate echo detection, and false-positive-free behavior with surrogate content\n- CI: 10 green checks on the PR\n\n## Risk checklist\n\n- [x] This change is backwards compatible\n- [x] This change has been tested with existing configurations\n- [ ] I have updated relevant documentation\n- [ ] Breaking changes (if any) are documented in Summary\n\nmerge-risk: low — pure refactor of two internal call sites from `String.slice()` to existing safe-slicing utility, no new dependencies, no config changes, no API surface changes, 10 green CI checks",
